            "<strong>Psychosis</strong> is a loss of contact with reality shown by delusions, hallucinations and disorganised thinking; it is a syndrome with many causes. <strong>Schizophrenia</strong> is the commonest chronic psychotic illness, affecting about 1 in 100 people in every country, beginning typically between 15 and 30 (earlier in men), and running a relapsing course in which about a third recover well, a third have recurrent episodes with reasonable function between, and a third remain disabled. It is not a split personality, not caused by bad parenting or a curse, and not untreatable: most people improve substantially on medication, and personal recovery — a trade, a family, a role — is possible for most.",
            "Symptoms fall into three groups. <strong>Positive symptoms</strong> are experiences added to normal life: <strong>delusions</strong> (persecutory — being poisoned, followed or bewitched; of reference; of control, with thoughts inserted, withdrawn or broadcast; grandiose; religious), <strong>hallucinations</strong> (most often auditory — voices talking about the person in the third person, commenting on his actions, or <strong>commanding</strong> him), and <strong>thought disorder</strong> (loosening of associations, derailment, neologisms, speech that cannot be followed). <strong>Negative symptoms</strong> are things taken away: <strong>flat affect</strong>, poverty of speech, <strong>apathy</strong> and lack of drive, anhedonia, <strong>social withdrawal</strong> and <strong>self-neglect</strong> — the man who sits alone all day, does not speak and has not bathed for weeks. They are mistaken by families for laziness and by nurses for depression, respond poorly to drugs, and cause most of the long-term disability. <strong>Cognitive symptoms</strong> — poor attention, memory and planning — are the reason a once-bright student cannot return to school even when the voices stop. <strong>Catatonia</strong> — stupor, mutism, posturing, waxy flexibility, or sudden excitement — is a medical emergency because the patient neither eats nor drinks.",
