            "Everyone is sad sometimes; <strong>depressive disorder</strong> is a persistent change lasting at least two weeks, most of the day nearly every day, that impairs function. The <strong>core symptoms</strong> are <strong>low mood</strong>, <strong>loss of interest and pleasure</strong> (anhedonia — the woman who no longer goes to church or enjoys her grandchildren) and <strong>fatigue</strong>. The <strong>biological symptoms</strong> are disturbed sleep — classically <strong>early morning waking</strong> two or more hours before usual, though many sleep more — <strong>poor appetite</strong> and weight loss (or the reverse), diurnal variation with mornings worst, loss of libido, and <strong>psychomotor retardation</strong> (slowed speech and movement) or agitation. The <strong>cognitive symptoms</strong> are poor concentration and memory, indecision, <strong>guilt</strong>, <strong>worthlessness</strong>, <strong>hopelessness</strong> about the future, and thoughts of death and suicide. In <strong>severe</strong> depression there may be psychotic features — nihilistic delusions ('my insides have rotted', 'I have ruined my family'), delusions of guilt and poverty, and accusing voices — and stupor, with refusal of food and fluid.",
