            "Once she is physically safe, the psychiatric assessment follows: the circumstances of the act (planned or impulsive, alone or where she would be found, a note, precautions against discovery, what she expected to happen, how she feels about surviving), current intent, mental illness, substances, supports and stressors, and a risk formulation. Before she leaves — and no one leaves from the medical ward without this — there is a <strong>safety plan</strong> written with her: her warning signs, her own coping strategies, people and places that distract, people she can tell, professionals and numbers to call, and <strong>how the means will be made safe</strong> at home (the pesticide removed, tablets held by a relative). Follow-up within a week is arranged and the family know the plan. <strong>Psychological support after the attempt</strong> — brief problem-solving, treatment of the depression, help with the debt or the marriage — addresses what drove it.",
