            "<strong>Bipolar affective disorder</strong> is a recurrent illness of episodes of mania or hypomania and, usually, episodes of depression, with normal mood between. It affects about 1 in 100, begins in the late teens or twenties, runs strongly in families, and is lifelong; untreated, episodes become more frequent. <strong>Mania</strong> is a week or more of <strong>elevated, expansive or irritable mood</strong> with overactivity: <strong>euphoria</strong> or irritability, <strong>grandiosity</strong> (he is a prophet, a millionaire, a minister), markedly <strong>reduced need for sleep</strong> ('I have not slept for four nights and I have never felt better'), <strong>pressure of speech</strong> (rapid, loud, impossible to interrupt), <strong>flight of ideas</strong> (racing from topic to topic by rhyme and association), distractibility, increased goal-directed activity, and <strong>disinhibition</strong> — spending and giving away money and property, sexual indiscretion, reckless driving, religious preaching in the street, quarrels and fights. Psychotic symptoms — grandiose or persecutory delusions — are common in severe mania. <strong>Hypomania</strong> is the same picture, milder, without psychosis and without severe impairment, and is often enjoyed by the patient and missed by everyone. The <strong>depressive episodes</strong> look like any severe depression and carry a high suicide risk.",
            "Mania can be caused or precipitated by <strong>antidepressants</strong> (a person with unrecognised bipolar disorder started on fluoxetine for depression switches into mania — the reason antidepressants are used with caution and usually with a mood stabiliser in bipolar depression), <strong>steroids</strong>, stimulants and tramadol, <strong>sleep deprivation</strong> (night shifts, exam weeks, a new baby), thyrotoxicosis, HIV and cerebral malaria. Postpartum psychosis is frequently a manic illness in a woman with bipolar vulnerability (Lesson 6.1).",
