            "<strong>Communication and limit-setting</strong>: short, simple, calm sentences; one request at a time; a consistent approach from all staff so he cannot play one against another; firm, clear, non-punitive <strong>limits</strong> on behaviour that endangers or intrudes on others ('You may not go into the women's dormitory. You can walk in the courtyard with me.'); no arguments, no humour at his expense, and no being drawn into his projects. <strong>Protection from consequences</strong>: with the family, secure his money, phone, car keys, land documents and signature during the episode, because the property he gives away and the contracts he signs while manic will not be returned when he recovers; and remember that a disinhibited woman on an open ward must be protected from sexual exploitation and its results — pregnancy and infection — by observation, not by blame. <strong>Medication</strong>: an antipsychotic (haloperidol, olanzapine, risperidone) and/or a benzodiazepine brings the acute episode under control within days; a <strong>mood stabiliser</strong> — lithium, valproate or carbamazepine — prevents the next one.",
