            "<strong>Lithium carbonate</strong> is the best-proven mood stabiliser: it prevents both manic and depressive relapse and reduces suicide. It has a <strong>narrow therapeutic index</strong> — the difference between a dose that works and a dose that poisons is small — so it is managed by <strong>serum levels</strong>: therapeutic <strong>0.6–1.2 mmol/L</strong> (some units aim for 0.6–1.0 for maintenance), caution <strong>1.2–1.5</strong>, toxic <strong>above 1.5</strong>, life-threatening above 2.0. The sample is taken <strong>12 hours after the last dose</strong> (a trough: the evening dose taken, the morning dose held until blood is drawn), five to seven days after starting or changing the dose and then every three to six months once stable, with <strong>urea, electrolytes and creatinine</strong> (lithium is excreted unchanged by the kidney and damages it over years) and <strong>thyroid function tests</strong> (lithium causes hypothyroidism — weight gain, tiredness, cold intolerance, a low mood that is mistaken for relapse — and is managed with thyroxine, not by stopping lithium) at baseline and every six to twelve months. An ECG, calcium and a pregnancy test are part of the baseline.",
            "Lithium is handled by the kidney like sodium. <strong>Anything that concentrates it is dangerous</strong>: <strong>dehydration</strong> from heat, fever, fasting or hard labour; <strong>vomiting and diarrhoea</strong> from gastroenteritis or malaria; a <strong>low salt diet</strong> or sudden salt loss; <strong>thiazide diuretics</strong> such as bendroflumethiazide and hydrochlorothiazide, and the <strong>hyponatraemia caused by diuretics</strong>; <strong>ACE inhibitors</strong> (lisinopril); <strong>NSAIDs</strong> — the <strong>ibuprofen</strong> and diclofenac sold at every kiosk; and <strong>renal impairment</strong>, in which the dose must be adjusted to creatinine clearance. Common, expected side effects at therapeutic levels are a <strong>fine tremor</strong> of the hands, thirst and <strong>polyuria</strong> (lithium blunts the kidney's response to ADH, a nephrogenic diabetes insipidus), weight gain, metallic taste, nausea, acne and worsening of psoriasis.",
