            "<strong>Absorption</strong> gets a drug into the blood (food, vomiting and antacids interfere); <strong>distribution</strong> carries it to the tissues (only the unbound fraction acts, so the malnourished patient with low albumin feels a 'normal' dose of a highly bound drug like valproate more strongly); <strong>metabolism</strong>, mostly in the liver, converts it to something excretable; <strong>excretion</strong>, mostly by the kidney, removes it. An oral dose is larger than an injected one because of <strong>first-pass metabolism</strong> — the swallowed drug passes through the liver before reaching the body. The <strong>half-life</strong> is the time for the level to fall by half; a drug given regularly reaches a <strong>steady state</strong> after about <strong>five half-lives</strong>, and takes five more to clear after it is stopped. That is why lithium levels are first checked after five to seven days, why fluoxetine (whose active metabolite has a half-life of about a week) lingers for weeks after stopping, and why a <strong>loading dose</strong> is used when steady state cannot be waited for. Before any dose of a renally cleared drug the question is the kidney — <strong>creatinine clearance</strong>, estimated from age, weight and creatinine — and before any hepatically cleared drug, the liver; the very old and the very young have less of both to spare.",
